WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.601
[music playing]

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:02.969
NARRATOR: And while
sharks have always

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:07.674
been present along this massive
shoreline, starting in 2010,

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:11.378
they become a problem.

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:14.414
More than 60 attacks
in just 10 years,

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:18.218
triple the number of incidents
from the preceding decade--

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:20.019
it's an unprecedented uptick.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:26.993
Shark attacks in Western
Australia previously were rare.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.828
Now they're almost
regular events.

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:35.935
NARRATOR: Not only that, the
spike includes a horrifying 11

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:41.708
fatalities, earning this tourist
haven a terrifying designation

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:45.345
as Australia's deadliest coast.

00:00:45.412 --> 00:00:47.046
[dramatic music]

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:48.882
TREVOR WHYTE: The amount
of vicious attacks--

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:49.682
there's no question.

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:52.552
It becomes talk of the town.

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:55.755
People are scared to
go back in the water.

00:00:55.822 --> 00:00:58.191
NARRATOR: With residents
demanding action,

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:00.093
authorities put
prevention measures

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.363
in place, including
aerial surveillance

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.365
and beach enclosures.

00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:10.103
But the attacks
just keep coming.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.306
So investigators take
up the case in a bid

00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:17.777
to stop the bloodshed and keep
it from recurring elsewhere.

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:19.846
BLAKE CHAPMAN: The more we can
research into what's happening,

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:22.115
then the more human
fatalities and human trauma

00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:24.551
we're going to be
able to prevent.

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:28.221
NARRATOR: So what's causing
the sharks of Western Australia

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:29.689
to suddenly go on a rampage?

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:37.363
Shark expert Johann
Gustafson believes

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.366
that a crucial first step
in solving this mystery

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.702
is to identify the culprit.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:44.938
So he turns to a
piece of evidence

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:47.240
from the attack on Elyse.

00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:49.943
So here we have a
fragment of tooth that

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:53.446
was lodged within the victim.

00:01:53.513 --> 00:01:56.983
NARRATOR: Gustafson thinks
this tooth must belong to one

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:00.887
of three species which are
responsible for the majority

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:03.423
of the attacks worldwide.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.226
The first is the tiger shark.

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:07.460
[dubstep music]

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:10.096
So here we have
a tiger shark jaw.

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:12.799
And we see on one
side of the jaw,

00:02:12.866 --> 00:02:14.901
the blades are
pointing to the left,

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:16.302
where on the opposite
side of the jaw,

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:18.938
they're pointing in
the opposite direction.

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:21.107
NARRATOR: This allows
tigers to rip their prey

00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:24.944
from both sides of their
teeth as they shake

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:26.346
their heads back and forth.

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:32.485
A second likely culprit
is the bull shark.

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:33.686
[dubstep music]

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:35.088
JOHANN GUSTAFSON: So
with our bull shark,

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:38.024
we have here a completely
different shape of tooth.

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:41.928
Quite pointed, serration
is quite sharp.

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:44.664
NARRATOR: The bull shark's
powerful pointed teeth

00:02:44.731 --> 00:02:47.100
enable it to slice
through its victim

00:02:47.166 --> 00:02:50.870
in one quick lethal motion.

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:55.208
But Gustafson believes the shape
of the tooth from Elyse's wound

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:57.777
indicates a different predator.

00:02:57.844 --> 00:03:01.681
With this tooth fragment,
it's perfectly triangular.

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:04.751
And it's quite
serrated on both sides.

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:07.587
Without doubt, I can conclude
this came from a white shark.

00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:08.888
[electronic music]

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:10.823
NARRATOR: Great
whites are the largest

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.092
predatory fish on the planet.

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:17.363
They use their powerful tails
to propel them through the water

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.367
at upwards of 25
miles per hour, often

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:23.102
striking their prey from below.

00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:32.879
But this identification isn't
limited to Elyse's attacker.

00:03:32.946 --> 00:03:37.116
Of the more than 60 attacks
where the species is known,

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:41.521
the majority of culprits
are white sharks.

00:03:41.588 --> 00:03:47.393
So what's causing great whites
here to act so strangely?

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:51.397
And could it be indicative
of a behavioral shift

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:55.034
happening across the globe?

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:58.571
Perhaps the answer relates
to a booming industry that's

00:03:58.638 --> 00:04:01.608
centered around
this iconic species,

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:05.345
one that occurs in
several locations but all

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:08.715
began down under--

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:11.751
cage diving.

00:04:11.818 --> 00:04:15.455
Shark expert Dr.
Dan Huber explains.

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:17.390
The way cage diving
operations work

00:04:17.457 --> 00:04:21.394
is that boats use chum to try
and attract sharks to the area.

00:04:21.461 --> 00:04:23.496
People then get into
cages, which are submerged

00:04:23.563 --> 00:04:27.433
in the water, to get much, much
closer to very dangerous sharks

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.635
than they would
otherwise be able to.

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:34.941
NARRATOR: Cage diving has been
a fixture of Australian tourism

00:04:35.008 --> 00:04:36.809
for decades.

00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:41.347
But around 2010,
something changed.

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:43.916
DAN HUBER: In 2010, when the
spike in shark attacks began,

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:46.252
the number of days that
chumming occurred on the water

00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:47.320
had doubled compared
to the previous year.

